          ":question/id": "3c835c9f-7022-5bdf-9b7d-f5a905e41275",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-42062",
          ":question/problem": "Solve the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-6y &=9 \\\\ -2x+4y &=-6 \\\\ x-2y &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+2y \\\\ y &=y,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        }
