            ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Already in Row Echelon Form"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096675,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096462,
              ":question/id": "239a008b-4c67-5334-8285-3c46b773fead",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4710",
              ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-z &=1 \\\\ 2x+y+3z &=4 \\\\ 5x+4y+z &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 7\\end{array} \\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3 \\\\ y &=-2 \\\\ z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=3,$ $y=-2,$ and $z=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 3,-2,0 \\rangle.$"
